ap black sets up and if  you are white  you should certainly avoid it  so the big question arrives now how we  are going to defend the b2 pawn  well the answer is very simple you are  going to play  queen to c2 not only defending  but setting up a cunning counter trap  and i can't tell you how many people has  fallen into this  as they don't recognize the difference  between  queen to b3 and queen to c2 and they  think  that oh another big mistake by white  i am going to play bishop f5 so that  he can't take my bishop and then i will  steamroll him on the queen side  but the matter of fact is  it is now black who is asking for the  trouble  before i move on i like you to spot the  difference  and find out the key move which creates  a huge problem for the black camp  are you ready let's start the key move  here is  d capture c5  so the first thing first if black  captured the spawn  then we are more than happy to lop off  this  free piece as queen now cannot go to b2  unless black get two moves  so that's right out of the equation  and that means black response is force  he has to take this queen  after that we will take black queen  and now black has two choices  the first option a captures b6 which is  most oftenly played  which i consider is a dubious try by  black  as after the simple move knight to a3  not only we are attacking the bishop but  in the next turn  this knight will sit on this juicy b5  square  which can no longer harass by the black  pieces  for example if black continue with bad  moves such as  either bishop to f5 or bishop to g6  in that case after knight to b5  now you can see the dark side of this  whole transaction  not only black has some horrible pawn  structure on the queen side  but there is already a fork looming on  the c7 square  and to be honest it is very awkward for  the black camp  for example if he plays rook to c8 then  we have bishop to c7  and then there is a huge pressure on the  b6 pawn  and the second move king to d7 is  absolutely very bad  as y doesn't require much imagination  he can clearly continue with bishop e2  castle  centralizing the two roo and then hit  back with the c4  where black king can find himself in a  huge trouble  at the d-file so all in all  at this position black has to play  bishop to e4  so that when this dominating knight  comes to the b5 square  he can immediately chop it off  okay bishop captures b5  so the threat is very obvious we want to  play knight to e5  and actually from here onwards the fun  begins  for the white camp  well to show you how dangerous this  position is  i like to show you a model game where a  2200 rated opponent  crushed a 2000 plus rated opponent  in just mere 19 moves  in the game black stopped knight to e5  with  knight to d7 well now some natural moves  from the buddha side  castle on the king side e6  trying to liberate the last piece  rook to d1 bishop to e7  and black is just one move away from the  castle  but guess what white didn't even give  time for that he lash out with  c4  well there is no good way to defend d5  so black plays d capture c4  and now here white can play knight to d4  and get a good position  but i think he choose the more  aggressive response  starting with rook 27  black must be in a panic state as he  cannot castle on the king's side  and after rook to c4 the c6 knight comes  into the huge pressure so he decided  to castle on the queen side connecting  the rook  defending d7 but missing the important  tactic  that is rook capture c4  well how are you going to defend this  blackjuice knight to b8  more pressure with rook to c1  rook to d5 counter-attacking the bishop  but very calm and good counter attacking  reply  knight to d4 and if you look at this  position  black is really out of the move  no wonder black made the critical error  from this position  continuing with rook h2d8  but just one move and black resigned  i wonder if you can spot it  did you find it let's see  the star move is bishop captures be it  bam  well black simply resign in the view of  king takes b8 and now bishop captures c6  and you can't take this bishop because  after knight captures c6  you are getting the rook on the d8 and  if not  then white emerge with a clear piece  advantage  so i think this game is a moral  demonstration what happens  if white continue with a captures  b6  probably more tasting reply here is  bishop captures b1  rook captures b1 and now a captures b6  at least black is generating the  pressure on the a2 pawn  well not so surprising white has some  cunning trap here as well  starting with bishop to b5  inviting black to get a pawn on a2  well apart from it if black tries other  moves such as  e6 or knight to d7 then  white has a very simple reply a3  and with the two bishop pair and the  fracture pawn structure  this is definitely great advantage  to the white camp to cut the long story  short  black has to accept this pawn with rook  captures a2  and after that we are going to continue  with  knight to e5 so as you can  see there is no way black can defend on  c6  and even after the base sequence as per  the engine  rook to a5 attacking the bishop bishop  capture c6  pawn takes c6 knight takes c6 attacking  the rook  black can't play rook 28 because white  has a simple threat namely  king tweet when rook to even where this  lack of peace development on the king's  side  paid heavy price to the black cam so  accordingly  the best response here is rook to c5  after that we are going to continue with  knight to b4  so at first sight it looks like there is  nothing going on in this position  and white is slightly better but see  yourself  after e6 liberating the other bishop  white has this very nice move rook to  even  so penetration on the second rank is on  the card  and there are two ways black can respond  to this  number one if we continue with rook to  c8  which attack the knight well time being  we can put our knight back to d3  and now after some natural moves bishop  to e7 and rook to a7  number one black cannot castle on the  king side  number two white has a very simple plan  including king to e2 double the rook on  the a file  or maybe create pressure on the b7 file  all together if you check this position  in any engine  even though material is equal white is  clearly  winning  but some of you will ask what about rook  to e5  isn't that stop any nuisance on the file  well yes it can except it cost  a whole pawn for the black cam as after  the following sequence  rook captures e5 pawn takes a5  and now knight to c6 are tackling the  pawn  okay that guy is completely helpless  so he has to push on but after king to  e2  in the next turn rook is going to munch  that pawn  the best black can do over here is a3  separate of a pawn but nope he can't  take on a3  the simple reason is after rook to even  suddenly black find to his horror that  he is going to lose a piece  and in fact the game  that's it guys i hope you enjoy and  learn this  wonderful counter trap against one of  the most aggressive setup by the black  remember play the move queen to c2  invite your opponent for bishop to f5 so  that  you can take on c5 and after the forcing  sequence  bishop capture c2 and pawn takes b6  no matter however black plays as  i have shown in this video with the  accurate play  it is white who has clearly better  chance  to get the full point thank you for  watching this video  feel free to like subscribe and comment  and i will meet you in my next episode  very soon bye and take care

